CHAPTER V : PHASE 1 - THE BARE-METAL BOMBE
===========================================================================

The first phase of our code is a digital tribute to Turing's Bombe. However, 
instead of waiting hours for mechanical rotors to spin, we execute the search 
in milliseconds using multi-threading and O(1) Memory Matrices.

[THE ABSTRACT CODE LOGIC: `brute_force_bombe` & `bombe_worker`]

1. Crib Dragging: The program searches the ciphertext for valid offsets where 
   the Crib does not clash with the Ciphertext (the Enigma flaw).
2. The Thread Pool: We unleash every CPU core available
   (`std::thread::hardware_concurrency()`). We divide the 11,881,376 possible
   rotor states evenly across these cores.
3. The Zero-Allocation Matrix: Inside the threads, we don't instantiate heavy 
   objects. We use a flat `FastMap` (a simple array of 26 bytes) to track 
   plugboard assumptions. 
4. The Bitwise Solver: `solve_graph_helper_fast` uses bitmasks (`uint64_t`) 
   to instantly traverse the logical graph. If a contradiction is found, it 
   bails out in nanoseconds.

// Abstract representation of the Digital Bombe's brain
bool solve_graph_helper_fast(FastMap& S, uint64_t remaining_mask, ...)
{
    // If all letters in the crib are verified, we found a True Stop!
    if (remaining_mask == 0) return true; 

    // Find the best letter to test next using bitwise operations
    // ...
    
    // Check for logical paradoxes (e.g., A is wired to B AND C)
    if (S.count(target) && S.get(target) != unknown) return false; // PARADOX!

    // If no paradox, recursively dive deeper into the logic tree
    return solve_graph_helper_fast(S_new, next_mask, ...);
}

When this phase finishes, we have narrowed down the 158 Quintillion
possibilities to a handful of "Mathematical Equivalents" (usually around 20-30
False Stops and 1 True Stop).

