CHAPTER VI : PHASE 1.5 & 2 - DARWIN'S STECKERBRETT

The Bombe cannot find the full Steckerbrett; it only finds fragments.
To solve the rest of the plugboard, we built a Genetic Algorithm (GA).

Why? Because traditional algorithms get stuck in "local maxima". If you
change one wire and the decryption gets worse, a standard algorithm gives up.
A Genetic Algorithm doesn't care; it explores the chaos.

[THE ABSTRACT CODE LOGIC: fast_genetic_eval & genetic_algorithm_steckerbrett]

1. Genesis: We create a population of 150 Chromosome objects. Each
chromosome holds a random plugboard wiring map.

2. Fitness Evaluation: Every chromosome decrypts the text. We score the text
to see how "German" it looks. (More on this in Chapter VII).

3. Survival & Crossover: We sort the population by fitness. The worst
performers are deleted from RAM. The elites are kept. We mix the maps of
the elites to create new children.

4. Mutation: To prevent inbreeding, we use a Mersenne Twister (mt19937) to
randomly zap chromosomes with cosmic rays, swapping random wires.

// Abstract representation of biological mutation
Chromosome mutate(Chromosome child, ...) {
    // Pick two random available letters
    char a = available[idx1];
    char b = available[idx2];
    
    // Sever their old connections
    child.map.erase(target_a); child.map.erase(target_b);
    
    // Splice the new DNA (Plug the new wire)
    if (a != b) {
        child.map[a] = b;
        child.map[b] = a;
    }
    return child;
}

The algorithm runs for 150 generations. You literally watch the text evolve
on your terminal from XQZ YTL into STRENG GEHEIM. But how does the code
know what is German?

