CHAPTER VIII : THE ORACLE (HIDDEN MARKOV MODELS & VITERBI)

The GA successfully decrypts the text, but military operators transmitted in
5-letter blocks (e.g., DASBO OTIST VERSE NKTNU LL). It's unreadable. We
need to inject spaces back where they belong.

To do this, we use a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) parsed via the Viterbi
Algorithm.

[THE ABSTRACT CODE LOGIC: markov_viterbi_segmentation]

Viterbi doesn't just guess spaces. It builds a mathematical "Trellis" (a
graph of probabilities). It sweeps through the string and asks two questions
at every single character:

1. Emission Probability: Is this chunk of letters a valid word?
("DASBO" = Garbage. "DAS" = High Probability).

2. Transition Probability: Does word B naturally follow word A in German?
("DAS" -> "BOOT" = Good. "DA" -> "SBO" = Terrible).

The algorithm calculates the best_path_score dynamically for every possible
segmentation. Finally, it uses backpointers to trace the ultimate path of
highest probability from the end of the message back to the beginning,
slicing the text with surgical accuracy.

// Abstract Viterbi Pathfinding
for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
    for (int k = 0; k < i; ++k) {
        string candidate_word = text.substr(k, i - k);
        
        // Calculate Emission (Is it a word?) & Transition (Grammar flow)
        double current_score = viterbi_trellis[k] + emission + transition;
        
        // Dynamic Programming: Save only the best path
        if (current_score > best_path_score) {
            best_path_score = current_score;
            best_prev_state = k; // Breadcrumb trail
        }
    }
}
// Follow breadcrumbs backwards to reconstruct spaces!

